"""Active-site investigation instructions, kept separate from numerical sheets."""

ACTIVE_SITE_TASK = """Determine what the source active-site titration establishes about the assayed molecule's functional amount and the denominator used for molecular activity. Start from the supplied sequence, selected source contents or snippets and assigned experimental question; neither a searchable name nor a candidate annotation is required. Establish the actual organism, molecule, construct, preparation, assay, conditions and endpoint. Identify intended and additional preparation changes. Tagged and tag-cleaved preparations assayed separately do not establish coexisting contributions or isolate a causal tag effect when purification also changes.

Select literal source measurements of protein mass concentration, the assumed molecular weight and its molecular basis, and the already estimated active-site concentration. Preserve nominal, estimated and measured meanings rather than upgrading a source estimate. A multimeric protein does not make its quoted molecular weight a multimer molecular weight: establish whether the source value is per monomer or per complex. Do not invent a complex-to-monomer coefficient. Establish that the mass concentration, molecular weight and titration refer to the same actual preparation and assay context; a concentration in a later kinetic dilution is a distinct quantity. Preserve useful individual estimates when this relationship is unknown or conflicting.

Distinguish the reported nominal titrant concentration from an independently standardized active titrant concentration. Chemical purity measured by LC/MS or an optical detector does not supply the molar active titrant fraction. Identify what supports a one-to-one titrant-to-site interpretation, such as the source's irreversible inhibition and adduct evidence, and distinguish that from an active-stock calibration. The source's fitted x-intercept may already represent active sites: do not divide that active-site estimate again by titrant stoichiometry. Separately establish whether one functional site per protein monomer supports interpreting sites per total monomer as an active monomer fraction. An unknown sites-per-monomer relationship leaves density useful while the biological fraction stays unknown. Preserve the raw reported density domain unchanged. With the supported one-site interpretation, the biological fraction explicitly intersects that domain with physical [0,1]; report that conditioning, and return unavailable when the intersection is empty. A point estimate above one is not clamped into a valid fraction.

Identify the normalization stage of any relevant rate: nominal total monomer, titrated active sites, no selected rate, unknown or conflicting. A conditional denominator conversion requires the same rate and same preparation and assay; an already active-normalized turnover must not receive the factor again. Keep the active fraction distinct from instrument recovery or an optical inner-filter correction. Preserve source-rounded percentages separately from fractions derived from the selected mass, molecular weight and active-site estimate. Do not silently reconcile rounding or substitute a percentage into the active concentration from another experiment.

Retain reported bounds, source-qualified approximation, uncertainty kinds and repetition basis. A regression SD is neither an SEM nor variability across independent preparations; repeated aliquots from one preparation do not establish independent preparation replication. Do not invent full fraction uncertainty from one source spread when mass, molecular-weight, titrant calibration, regression and covariance contributions are unresolved. Preserve unknown and conflicting meanings explicitly. Source quotations establish custody, not the scientific truth of an interpretation. Nominate narrow context spans where possible, retaining necessary scientific context even when it contains numbers. The deterministic reader performs unit conversion and arithmetic from source NUMBER and UNIT selections; computed values are not extraction inputs. Return exactly the parent's requested typed components with their source meanings, origin, uncertainty and dependence. Revise subsequent investigation choices from actual outcomes under the ordinary episode controller."""

ACTIVE_SITE_OPERATION_DESCRIPTION = "Populate preparation-specific active-site titration amount components from source NUMBER/UNIT selections: mass concentration, assumed molecular weight, total monomer concentration, estimated active sites, density, conditional one-site active fraction and denominator conversion. Preserve nominal versus standardized titrant calibration, stoichiometry, molecular basis, preparation/assay linkage, normalization stage, source uncertainty and reported percentages. No correction is applied to rates and no reliability or annotation-credit aggregate is made. Return requested_active_site_components through literature_active_site_cells."
